==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=Palooka}}

===Etymology===
{{unk|en|title=Used }} in the [[US]] since the 1920s, originally primarily of boxers. Popularized by Jack Conway of ''[[w:Variety (magazine)|Variety]],''<ref>''Esquire,'' September 1936</ref><ref>{{w|H. L. Mencken}}, 1945 supplement to ''{{w|The American Language}},'' reviewed in [http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,792388,00.html Books: Alphabet Soup], ''TIME,'' Monday, Aug. 27, 1945</ref> who also popularized {{m|en|baloney}} and {{m|en|bimbo}}. Further popularized by {{w|Ham Fisher}} in his comic strip ''{{w|Joe Palooka}}'', about a boxer (published in newspapers since 1930, particularly popular in 1940s).<ref>''Cassell’s Dictionary Of Slang.'' Jonathon Green. Cassel & Co., 1998. {{ISBN|0-304-35167-9}}</ref>

